A||||3||Mark||Valve Seal Replacement||||5||
Z||000000||Mark||08-16-2000||10:28 PM||||I'm looking for any suggestions or tips (encouragement is always welcome too) for installing new valve stem seals in a V6.  The heads are on the engine. I have compressed air and associated fittings to pressurize the cylinder and hold the valves up.  Some questions though....<P>I read somewhere that someone suggested feeding some nylon rope into the spark plug hole then hand cranking the engine until the piston squeezes the rope up against the closed valves.  This method seems to have real merit (no way valves can fall into cyliners!!!)  Anybody tried this method?<P>Should the rocker nuts be replaced?  Anything else?<P>O-ring seals must be slipped on after compressing spring, right?  Sounds like a tricky operation.  I assume you have to compress the crap out of the spring to expose the second groove, but not so much as to squish the intake seals?<P>I expect to remove the upper and middle plenum to get the valve covers off.  Any words of wisdom?  How about the fuel rail? Does it have to go also?<P>Any web sites describing this job? <P>Best pain relief for sore back?<P>Thanks guys,<P>Mark||||reg||5
Z||000001||2birds||08-17-2000||02:39 AM||||Try this link.  It rules, IMHO.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/</A> <P>It has an article on this subject.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||08-17-2000||08:58 AM||||Yes- I used the nylon rope trick, except I used an old appliance cord.  It works great.  In my opinion, there's no good reason to use compressed air, even though that's the method you always read about.  Unless you <B>enjoy</B> threading the air fitting into spark plug holes w/o crossthreading, worrying about the crank turning if you don't get it on TDC, and somebody "borrowing your air for a sec!"<P>I have never had to replace the rocker arm nuts - I figure, as long as I'm confident the self-locking feature still works, they're okay.  On the other hand, I'm kind of a cheapskate - you might want to price the new nuts, and from there, decide whether it's worth it to <B>you</B>.  Bottom line: if it makes you feel better, replace them.     <P>You do have to squash the valve spring pretty far to get the seal into the 2nd groove.  I wrote down a procedure that worked for me, but don't have it with me today.  <P>Fuel rail has to be removed to pull off the intermediate manifold - so you can get the valve covers off.  At least one of the manuals suggests that  you can remove the valve cover w/o pulling the intermediate manifold, but I guess I'm not smart enough to see how that can happen.  <P>Like most other worthwhile jobs, it's not too difficult, but it is time-consuming.  Take your time, clean things as you go, and you'll do fine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !<BR>          <BR>  ||||reg||6
Z||000003||Mark||08-17-2000||08:15 PM||||Thanks, guys.  I'll probably use the rope trick.  There's no way to lose the valve in the cylinder when the piston is at the top!||||reg||1
